"field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978897/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c754dc690a836b9ca4b87b9b05ffe34a8684e62be8a66d213448616d0770ad7"} {"question" "q-29222" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}"] ["math" "x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}"] ["math" "x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}"] ["math" "x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}"] ["math" "x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978897/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c754dc690a836b9ca4b87b9b05ffe34a8684e62be8a66d213448616d0770ad7"} {"question" "q-29222" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978897/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c754dc690a836b9ca4b87b9b05ffe34a8684e62be8a66d213448616d0770ad7"} {"question" "q-80200" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $7\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-5=-5-3\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ for $-\\pi <x<\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978897/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c754dc690a836b9ca4b87b9b05ffe34a8684e62be8a66d213448616d0770ad7"} {"question" "q-80200" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-5 & =-5-3\\operatorname{cot}⁡x \\\\ 10\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can't find an equivalent equation involving tangent, since this would lead to us dividing by $0:$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{1}{0}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can instead find an equivalent equation using the fact that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=0\n$$\n\nIn this case, we can find the principal solution as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value lies inside the desired domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nTo find more solutions, we subtract integer multiples of $\\pi $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\pi  \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our solutions are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978897/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c754dc690a836b9ca4b87b9b05ffe34a8684e62be8a66d213448616d0770ad7"} {"question" "q-80200" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978897/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6612beaf8e0032d1dc5cd7c9320856b8cdd9c8c6f14b2e8b6858738690f64225"} {"question" "q-80200" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978897/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c754dc690a836b9ca4b87b9b05ffe34a8684e62be8a66d213448616d0770ad7"} {"question" "q-80200" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{3{\\pi }^{2}}{4}"] ["math" "-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{16}"] ["math" "-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{9}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978897/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c754dc690a836b9ca4b87b9b05ffe34a8684e62be8a66d213448616d0770ad7"} {"question" "q-80200" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978897/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c754dc690a836b9ca4b87b9b05ffe34a8684e62be8a66d213448616d0770ad7"} {"question" "q-330722" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the two solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\sqrt{18}=3\\sqrt{2}$ for ${0}^{∘}<x<{360}^{∘}.$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$ \n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$ and $x=$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978897/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c754dc690a836b9ca4b87b9b05ffe34a8684e62be8a66d213448616d0770ad7"} {"question" "q-330722" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\sqrt{18} & =3\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =3\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{18} \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can't find an equivalent equation involving tangent, since this would lead to us dividing by $0:$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{1}{0}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can instead find an equivalent equation using the fact that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=0\n$$\n\nIn this case, we can find the principal solution as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡0 \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={90}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={90}^{∘}.$\n\nTo find more solutions, we add integer multiples of ${180}^{∘}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =x+{180}^{∘}={90}^{∘}+{180}^{∘}={270}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, our solutions are $x={90}^{∘}$ and $x={270}^{∘}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978897/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c754dc690a836b9ca4b87b9b05ffe34a8684e62be8a66d213448616d0770ad7"} {"question" "q-330722" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978897/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6612beaf8e0032d1dc5cd7c9320856b8cdd9c8c6f14b2e8b6858738690f64225"} {"question" "q-330722" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978897/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c754dc690a836b9ca4b87b9b05ffe34a8684e62be8a66d213448616d0770ad7"} {"question" "q-330722" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "90"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978897/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c754dc690a836b9ca4b87b9b05ffe34a8684e62be8a66d213448616d0770ad7"} {"question" "q-330722" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978897/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c754dc690a836b9ca4b87b9b05ffe34a8684e62be8a66d213448616d0770ad7"} {"question" "q-330722" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "270"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978897/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c754dc690a836b9ca4b87b9b05ffe34a8684e62be8a66d213448616d0770ad7"} {"question" "q-120643" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $3=3-\\frac{7\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{5}$ for ${0}^{∘}<x<{360}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978897/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c754dc690a836b9ca4b87b9b05ffe34a8684e62be8a66d213448616d0770ad7"} {"question" "q-120643" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =3-\\frac{7\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{5} \\\\ 0 & =-\\frac{7\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{5} \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can't find an equivalent equation involving tangent, since this would lead to us dividing by $0:$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{1}{0}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can instead find an equivalent equation using the fact that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=0\n$$\n\nIn this case, we can find the principal solution as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡0 \\\\ x & ={90}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value lies inside the desired domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={90}^{∘}.$\n\nTo find more solutions, we add integer multiples of ${180}^{∘}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}+{180}^{∘} \\\\  & ={90}^{∘}+{180}^{∘} \\\\  & ={270}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our solutions are $x={90}^{∘},{270}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ is \n\n$$\n90\\cdot 270=24\\,300.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978897/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c754dc690a836b9ca4b87b9b05ffe34a8684e62be8a66d213448616d0770ad7"} {"question" "q-120643" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978897/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6612beaf8e0032d1dc5cd7c9320856b8cdd9c8c6f14b2e8b6858738690f64225"} {"question" "q-120643" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978897/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c754dc690a836b9ca4b87b9b05ffe34a8684e62be8a66d213448616d0770ad7"} {"question" "q-120643" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "16\\,200"] ["math" "24\\,300"] ["math" "27\\,000"] ["math" "48\\,600"] ["math" "8\\,100"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978897/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c754dc690a836b9ca4b87b9b05ffe34a8684e62be8a66d213448616d0770ad7"} {"question" "q-120643" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "24\\,300"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978897/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c754dc690a836b9ca4b87b9b05ffe34a8684e62be8a66d213448616d0770ad7"} {"question" "q-274769" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the two solutions to the equation $4\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+3=3+\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ for $-\\pi <x<\\pi .$\n\n**In ascending order,** $x=$ \n{{field-1}}\nand $x=$\n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978897/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c754dc690a836b9ca4b87b9b05ffe34a8684e62be8a66d213448616d0770ad7"} {"question" "q-274769" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+3 & =3+\\operatorname{cot}⁡x \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can't find an equivalent equation involving tangent, since this would lead to us dividing by $0:$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{1}{0}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can instead find an equivalent equation using the fact that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=0\n$$\n\nIn this case, we can find the principal solution as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value lies inside the desired domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nTo find more solutions, we subtract integer multiples of $\\pi $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\pi  \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, our solutions are $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978897/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c754dc690a836b9ca4b87b9b05ffe34a8684e62be8a66d213448616d0770ad7"} {"question" "q-274769" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978897/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6612beaf8e0032d1dc5cd7c9320856b8cdd9c8c6f14b2e8b6858738690f64225"} {"question" "q-274769" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978897/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c754dc690a836b9ca4b87b9b05ffe34a8684e62be8a66d213448616d0770ad7"} {"question" "q-274769" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{\\pi}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978897/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c754dc690a836b9ca4b87b9b05ffe34a8684e62be8a66d213448616d0770ad7"} {"question" "q-274769" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978897/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c754dc690a836b9ca4b87b9b05ffe34a8684e62be8a66d213448616d0770ad7"} {"question" "q-274769" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\pi}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978897/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c754dc690a836b9ca4b87b9b05ffe34a8684e62be8a66d213448616d0770ad7"} {"question" "q-330707" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the two solutions to the equation $2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+7=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+7$ for $0<x<2\\pi .$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$ \n{{field-1}}\nand $x=$ \n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978897/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c754dc690a836b9ca4b87b9b05ffe34a8684e62be8a66d213448616d0770ad7"} {"question" "q-330707" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+7 & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+7 \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can't find an equivalent equation involving tangent, since this would lead to us dividing by $0:$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{1}{0}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can instead find an equivalent equation using the fact that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=0\n$$\n\nIn this case, we can find the principal solution as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value lies inside the desired domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nTo find more solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}+\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, our solutions are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978897/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c754dc690a836b9ca4b87b9b05ffe34a8684e62be8a66d213448616d0770ad7"} {"question" "q-330707" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978897/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6612beaf8e0032d1dc5cd7c9320856b8cdd9c8c6f14b2e8b6858738690f64225"} {"question" "q-330707" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978897/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c754dc690a836b9ca4b87b9b05ffe34a8684e62be8a66d213448616d0770ad7"} {"question" "q-330707" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\pi}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978897/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c754dc690a836b9ca4b87b9b05ffe34a8684e62be8a66d213448616d0770ad7"} {"question" "q-330707" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978897/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c754dc690a836b9ca4b87b9b05ffe34a8684e62be8a66d213448616d0770ad7"} {"question" "q-330707" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3\\pi}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978897/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c754dc690a836b9ca4b87b9b05ffe34a8684e62be8a66d213448616d0770ad7"} {"question" "e-3537" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $x_1$ and $x_2$ are the solutions to the equation $3\\cot x + 3\\sqrt{3}=0$ for $0^\\circ \\leq x <  360^\\circ,$ what is the numerical value of $x_1\\cdot x_2?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978897/example-3537.json" "file_sha256" "06e0cac8ba26cd1decc419909b5909b4545062b1c9acabe6906317d9d801ee76"} {"question" "e-3537" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate $\\cot x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+3\\sqrt{3} & =0 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =-3\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =-\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\cot{x}=\\dfrac{1}{\\tan{x}}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\tan{x} =-\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}=-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})=-{30}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-30^\\circ$ lies outside the given domain, it is not a solution. However, we can generate solutions by adding integer multiples of $180^\\circ$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =x+{180}^{∘} \\\\  & =-{30}^{∘}+{180}^{∘} \\\\  & ={150}^{∘} \\\\  \\\\ {x}_{2} & =x+{360}^{∘} \\\\  & =-{30}^{∘}+{360}^{∘} \\\\  & ={330}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=150^\\circ, 330^\\circ.$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of $x_1 \\cdot x_2$ is $150 \\cdot 330 = 49\\,500.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978897/example-3537.json" "file_sha256" "06e0cac8ba26cd1decc419909b5909b4545062b1c9acabe6906317d9d801ee76"} {"question" "e-4266" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $x_1$ and $x_2$ are the solutions to the equation  $2\\cot x =9 - \\cot x$ for $0 \\leq x <                         2\\pi,$ what is the value of $x_1\\cdot x_2,$ rounded to one decimal place?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978897/example-4266.json" "file_sha256" "61a7d7536b61d4468e18af2889f5951d8ed059d888ab72e5323813461e87ae96"} {"question" "e-4266" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate $\\cot x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =9-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =9 \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\cot{x}=\\dfrac{1}{\\tan{x}}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\tan{x} =\\dfrac{1}{3} .\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\text{arctan}(\\frac{1}{3})=0.321\\,750≈0.321\\,8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $0.321\\,8$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is $x_1 = 0.321\\,8.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}+\\pi  \\\\  & =\\pi +0.321\\,8 \\\\  & =3.463\\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the value of $x_1\\cdot x_2$ is\n\n$$\n0.321\\,8 \\cdot 3.463\\,4 \\approx 1.1\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978897/example-4266.json" "file_sha256" "61a7d7536b61d4468e18af2889f5951d8ed059d888ab72e5323813461e87ae96"} {"question" "e-3539" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $x_1$ and $x_2$ are the solutions to the equation $3\\cot x + 3\\sqrt{3}=0$ for $-180^\\circ <                      x <                      180^\\circ,$ what is the numerical value of $x_1\\cdot x_2?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978897/example-3539.json" "file_sha256" "b51aeed79463b3f24f7d5e985e5a6c1dff85578438ef323263a68fa8fa86edd1"} {"question" "e-3539" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\cot x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+3\\sqrt{3} & =0 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =-3\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =-\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\cot{x}=\\dfrac{1}{\\tan{x}}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\tan{x} =-\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}=-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})=-{30}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-30^\\circ$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is $x_1 =-30^\\circ .$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of $180^\\circ$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}+{180}^{∘} \\\\  & =-{30}^{∘}+{180}^{∘} \\\\  & ={150}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x= -30^\\circ, 150^\\circ .$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of $x_1\\cdot x_2$ is \n\n$$\n-30\\cdot(150) = -4\\,500.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978897/example-3539.json" "file_sha256" "b51aeed79463b3f24f7d5e985e5a6c1dff85578438ef323263a68fa8fa86edd1"} {"question" "e-7699" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $x_1$ and $x_2$ are the solutions to the equation $3- 2\\cot x =3$ for $0^\\circ <                           x <                           360^\\circ,$ what is the numerical value of $x_1\\cdot x_2?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978897/example-7699.json" "file_sha256" "a9811ad197611643f5e1eb3b793f9df9a9849e9b16a26a9683c10eb8151c858d"} {"question" "e-7699" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate $\\cot x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =3 \\\\ -2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can't find an equivalent equation involving tangent, since this would lead to us dividing by $0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\tan x = \\dfrac{1}{0} = \\textrm{undefined}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can instead find an equivalent equation using the fact that $\\cot x = \\dfrac{\\cos x}{\\sin x}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cos x}{\\sin x}=0\n$$\n\nIn this case, we can find the principal solution as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\cos x &=0\\[5pt]\nx &= \\arccos 0 \\[5pt]\nx &= 90^\\circ\n}\n$$\n\nSince this value lies inside the desired domain, our first solution is $x_1 = 90^\\circ.$\n\nTo find more solutions, we add integer multiples of $180^\\circ$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}+{180}^{∘} \\\\  & ={90}^{∘}+{180}^{∘} \\\\  & ={270}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our solutions are $x=90^\\circ, 270^\\circ.$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of $x_1\\cdot x_2$ is \n\n$$\n90 \\cdot(270) = 24\\,300.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978897/example-7699.json" "file_sha256" "a9811ad197611643f5e1eb3b793f9df9a9849e9b16a26a9683c10eb8151c858d"}] "usage" {"e-3539" [] "e-7699" [] "e-3537" [] "q-29242" [] "e-4266" []} "basis" 838 "retractions" [[17592186115627 :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nGiven that $x_{1}$ and $x_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $5\\cot x = 11 + \\cot x$ for $0^{∘} \\le x < 360^{∘}$, what is the numerical value of $x_{1} \\cdot x_{2}$, rounded to the nearest integer?"] [17592186114927 :question/problem "Given that $x_1$ and $x_2$ are the solutions to the equation $3\\cot x + 3\\sqrt{3}=0$ for $0^\\circ \\leq x < 360^\\circ$, what is the numerical value of $x_1\\cdot x_2$?"] [17592186114927 :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate $\\cot x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3\\cot x + 3\\sqrt{3} &= 0 \\\\\n3\\cot x &= -3\\sqrt{3} \\\\\n\\cot x &= -\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\\n\\cot x &= -\\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that\n\n$$\n\\cot{x}=\\dfrac{1}{\\tan{x}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\tan{x} =-\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}=-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= \\arctan (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}) =-30^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-30^\\circ$ lies outside the given domain, it is not a solution. However, we can generate solutions by adding integer multiples of $180^\\circ$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1} &= x + 180^{∘} \\\\\n&=-30^{∘} + 180^{∘} \\\\\n&= 150^{∘} \\\\\nx_{2} &= x + 360^{∘} \\\\\n&=-30^{∘} + 360^{∘} \\\\\n&= 330^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are\n\n$$\nx=150^\\circ, 330^\\circ\n$$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of $x_1 \\cdot x_2$ is\n\n$$\n150 \\cdot 330 = 49\\,500\n$$"] [17592186114970 :question/problem "Given that $x_1$ and $x_2$ are the solutions to the equation $2\\cot x =9 - \\cot x$ for $0 \\leq x < 2\\pi$, what is the value of $x_1\\cdot x_2$, rounded to one decimal place?"] [17592186114970 :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate $\\cot x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\cot x &= 9 - \\cot x \\\\\n3\\cot x &= 9 \\\\\n\\cot x &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that\n\n$$\n\\cot{x}=\\dfrac{1}{\\tan{x}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\tan{x} =\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx = arctan(\\frac{1}{3}) = 0.321750 \\approx 0.3218\n$$\n\nSince $0.321\\,8$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is\n\n$$\nx_1 = 0.321\\,8\n$$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{2} &= x_{1} + π \\\\\n&= π + 0.3218 \\\\\n&= 3.4634\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the value of $x_1\\cdot x_2$ is\n\n$$\n0.321\\,8 \\cdot 3.463\\,4 \\approx 1.1\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."] [17592186114928 :question/problem "Given that $x_1$ and $x_2$ are the solutions to the equation $3\\cot x + 3\\sqrt{3}=0$ for $-180^\\circ < x < 180^\\circ$, what is the numerical value of $x_1\\cdot x_2$?"] [17592186114928 :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\cot x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3\\cot x + 3\\sqrt{3} &= 0 \\\\\n3\\cot x &= -3\\sqrt{3} \\\\\n\\cot x &= -\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\\n\\cot x &= -\\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that\n\n$$\n\\cot{x}=\\dfrac{1}{\\tan{x}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\tan{x} =-\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}=-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= \\arctan (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}) =-30^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-30^\\circ$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is\n\n$$\nx_1 =-30^\\circ\n$$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of $180^\\circ$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{2} &= x_{1} + 180^{∘} \\\\\n&=-30^{∘} + 180^{∘} \\\\\n&= 150^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are\n\n$$\nx= -30^\\circ, 150^\\circ\n$$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of $x_1\\cdot x_2$ is\n\n$$\n-30\\cdot(150) = -4\\,500\n$$"] [17592186115055 :question/problem "Given that $x_1$ and $x_2$ are the solutions to the equation $3- 2\\cot x =3$ for $0^\\circ < x < 360^\\circ$, what is the numerical value of $x_1\\cdot x_2$?"] [17592186115055 :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate $\\cot x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3 - 2\\cot x &= 3 \\\\\n- 2\\cot x &= 0 \\\\\n\\cot x &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can't find an equivalent equation involving tangent, since this would lead to us dividing by $0$:\n\n$$\n\\tan x = \\dfrac{1}{0} = \\textrm{undefined}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can instead find an equivalent equation using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\cot x = \\dfrac{\\cos x}{\\sin x}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cos x}{\\sin x}=0\n$$\n\nIn this case, we can find the principal solution as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\cos x &=0 \\\\[5pt] x &= \\arccos 0 \\\\[5pt] x &= 90^\\circ \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value lies inside the desired domain, our first solution is\n\n$$\nx_1 = 90^\\circ\n$$\n\nTo find more solutions, we add integer multiples of $180^\\circ$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{2} &= x_{1} + 180^{∘} \\\\\n&= 90^{∘} + 180^{∘} \\\\\n&= 270^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our solutions are\n\n$$\nx=90^\\circ, 270^\\circ\n$$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of $x_1\\cdot x_2$ is\n\n$$\n90 \\cdot(270) = 24\\,300\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186113163 17592186113164 17592186113165 17592186113166 17592186113167] "immutable_fields" [[17592186113162 :answer-field/correct] [17592186113162 :answer-field/id] [17592186113162 :answer-field/key] [17592186113162 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
